INT. VICTORIAN CHEMIST SHOP - AFTER HOURS - NIGHT

Rows of blue glass bottles gleam like sleeping eyes. Mahogany drawers labeled in copperplate. A brass scale, a mortar and pestle. A single gas lamp hisses above the counter.

MR. EZEKIEL FAIRBROTHER (60s, black apron, half-moon spectacles) stands behind the counter, wiping a pipette with a cloth. His apprentice, NELLIE QUINTANILLA-GRAY (teens, ink-stained cuffs, a braid pinned up), sweeps the floor too slowly. And by the door, shaking rain from his umbrella, stands DR. AUGUSTUS HELLEBORE (50s, silk hat, black bag).

                    AUGUSTUS
          Fairbrother. A word.

                    EZEKIEL
          Doctor. We are closed.

                    AUGUSTUS
          I am aware.

                    EZEKIEL
          Then you will understand if I say good night.

                    AUGUSTUS
          I want to know about the foxglove.

Nellie stops sweeping. Ezekiel folds the cloth in thirds and lays it on the counter.

                    EZEKIEL
          Foxglove is a common remedy.

                    AUGUSTUS
          In the quantity you dispensed to Mrs. Pennywhistle?

                    EZEKIEL
          I dispensed what was prescribed.

                    AUGUSTUS
          By whom?

                    EZEKIEL
          By you.

                    AUGUSTUS
          I prescribed a pinch.

                    EZEKIEL
          You prescribed a teaspoon, Doctor. In your own hand.

                    AUGUSTUS
          I did no such thing.

                    NELLIE
          (quietly)
          I have the slip, sir.

Both men turn. Nellie reaches under the counter and draws out a folded paper. Her hand is steady. She lays it flat.

                    AUGUSTUS
          That is a forgery.

                    NELLIE
          It is your signature.

                    AUGUSTUS
          Child, you do not know what you are saying.

                    NELLIE
          I know my letters, sir. I learned them from Mr. Fairbrother.

Ezekiel nods, slowly. Augustus's face changes, the polite mask slipping, and beneath it something older and colder looks out. His fingers close around the handle of his black bag.

                    AUGUSTUS
          Fairbrother, you will regret this.

                    EZEKIEL
          Doctor, I have regretted many things. This is not one of them.

Augustus stares at the paper. Then he snaps up his umbrella, turns, and strides out into the rain. The door slams. The bell above it jangles for a long time.

Nellie lets out a breath. Ezekiel takes up his cloth again and resumes wiping the pipette.

                    EZEKIEL (CONT'D)
          You were right to keep it.

                    NELLIE
          You taught me that, too.
